In this lesson we'll look at how to pick the right diagram when you're dealing with two events happening together. When you've got more than one random quantity, the way you draw it really matters, because a good diagram keeps the working tidy. There are four common ways to go. First, there's a list of ordered pairs, where you just write out every possible combination in words, like listing every coin and die result one by one. Second, you can turn that list into a sample grid, with the rows for one event and the columns for the other, so every cell is one outcome. Third, there are Venn diagrams, which use two overlapping circles inside a big rectangle. The whole rectangle stands for the sample space, one whole circle is your first event, the other circle is your second event, and where they overlap is where both happen at once. You can read off the probability of each event straight from the circle. Fourth, there's the tree diagram, where each branch is a stage of the experiment. You multiply along the branches to get the chance of one full path, and add them up when you want the chance of an event that can happen several different ways. Now let's see it in action.